{
  "dom_id": "step-q80703",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/da6318e1b1b8123845993da64cea95f640b0cb67ad94bb183bbbe9e5b1bd7b89.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/8f8ffabcc5b03b1f1b5856f4eb187137d1bf84043e47a43f6777df7b267853ec.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =xy-4x=x(y-4) \\\\ {y}^{\u2032} & =x(y-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=4$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>4$ | $\u2198$\n\n$(-)$ | $\u2197$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<4$ | $\u2197$\n\n$(+)$ | $\u2198$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=xy-4x=x(y-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}=xy-4x.$",
